<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Types of Load Balancers</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f4f6f9;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        h1 {
            text-align: center;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }
        h2 {
            color: #34495e;
            margin-top: 25px;
        }
        .section {
            background: #ffffff;
            padding: 15px;
            margin: 15px 0;
            border-left: 6px solid #e67e22;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        ul {
            margin-top: 10px;
        }
        table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin-top: 20px;
            background: #ffffff;
        }
        th, td {
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            padding: 10px;
            text-align: left;
        }
        th {
            background-color: #e67e22;
            color: white;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Types of Load Balancers</h1>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>1️⃣ Based on OSI Layer</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Layer 4 Load Balancer</b> – Works at Transport Layer (IP & Port based routing).</li>
            <li><b>Layer 7 Load Balancer</b> – Works at Application Layer (URL, Header based routing).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>2️⃣ Based on Deployment Type</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Hardware Load Balancer</b> – Physical device used in data centers.</li>
            <li><b>Software Load Balancer</b> – Installed as software (NGINX, HAProxy).</li>
            <li><b>Cloud Load Balancer</b> – Managed by cloud providers (AWS, Azure, GCP).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>3️⃣ Traffic Distribution Algorithms</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Round Robin</b> – Distributes requests sequentially.</li>
            <li><b>Least Connections</b> – Routes to server with fewer active connections.</li>
            <li><b>IP Hash</b> – Routes based on client IP address.</li>
            <li><b>Weighted Round Robin</b> – More powerful servers receive more traffic.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>4️⃣ Special Types</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Global Load Balancer</b> – Distributes traffic across multiple regions.</li>
            <li><b>Internal Load Balancer</b> – Used inside private networks only.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Works On</th>
            <th>Use Case</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Layer 4</td>
            <td>Transport Layer</td>
            <td>Fast routing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Layer 7</td>
            <td>Application Layer</td>
            <td>Smart routing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Hardware</td>
            <td>Physical Device</td>
            <td>Enterprise data centers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Software</td>
            <td>Server Application</td>
            <td>Cloud / On-premise</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Cloud</td>
            <td>Managed Service</td>
            <td>Scalable applications</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

</body>
</html>
